<DocLayout
  title="Configuration"
  description="The Sarthi Studio config file, environment variables, and saved settings for providers, routers, and the workspace."
>
  <p>
    Studio keeps its configuration in a JSON file at
    <code>~/.config/SAARTHI-STUDIO/config.json</code> and its databases under
    <code>~/.local/share/SAARTHI-STUDIO/</code>. Most settings are edited in
    the Settings UI; the sections below cover what you can configure and
    where.
  </p>

  <h2>Providers and routers</h2>
  <p>
    Provider presets are twelve OpenAI-compatible endpoints: FreeLLMAPI,
    OmniRoute, Hugging Face, Groq, Cerebras, Gemini, OpenAI, OpenRouter,
    Mistral, Cloudflare, Together, and Novita. Pick a preset, paste the
    endpoint's API key into the router, and test the connection. Studio
    never stores upstream provider keys — the router owns them.
  </p>
  <p>
    With FreeLLMAPI, Studio can manage the router lifecycle: it starts the
    router when it is stopped and stops it on exit <em>only if Studio
    started it</em>. The router console exposes the provider catalog, the
    routing strategy, analytics, and logs over loopback.
  </p>

  <h2>Local models</h2>
  <p>
    The Local AI panel detects your hardware, lists local models, and pulls
    new ones with progress. A local model needs no router key. Prefix an
    embedding model with <code>local:</code> to use a local Ollama model for
    semantic search.
  </p>

  <h2>Environment variables</h2>
  <p>
    A few settings can be set with environment variables. For example, to
    run on a different port:
    <code>SAARTHI_STUDIO_PORT=9000 SAARTHI-STUDIO</code>. See the
    configuration section in the repository's README and the
    <a href={withBase('/docs/getting-started/')}>Getting started</a>
    page for the exact variables.
  </p>

  <h2>Where settings are stored</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Config file: <code>~/.config/SAARTHI-STUDIO/config.json</code></li>
    <li>Database: <code>~/.local/share/SAARTHI-STUDIO/studio.db</code></li>
    <li>Router logs, analytics, and provider catalogs: in the local router console over loopback.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Honest limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Settings are stored as JSON on disk; protect the directory as you
      would any file with your model keys.
    </li>
    <li>
      Environment variables are a shortcut, not a replacement for the
      Settings UI — the UI is the supported editing surface.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocLayout>
